  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 239 AM EDT FRI SEP 18 2026\n\nUpper level ridging over the southeast CONUS will continue weaken as \nwe move into the the long term period. Largely zonal flow aloft will \nbe positioned just to our north at the start of the period. Multiple \nweak impulses moving through the flow will help to drive it very \nslowly southward as the upper high breaks down. By midweek, some \nsemblance of an upper low is forecast to evolve near the Great \nLakes, pushing faster flow aloft even further south for late week. \n\nAt the surface, this will eventually drive a significant cold front \nsouthward. This boundary should start the long term period to our \nnorth, near the Ohio River or further north, with its true position \npossibly somewhat muddled by prior convection. There is potential \nfor some of this convection to feed off of our heat and humidity on \nthis side of the front and make it south into our area in the \nSaturday night through Monday time frame, but confidence in details \nis very low due to mesoscale influences which would be in play. The \ngreatest POP looks to be as the front pushes through. Models have \nnot come into agreement yet, with cold fropa timing likely to be in \nthe Tue-Wed time frame. \n\nNoticeably cooler air should arrive behind the front as sprawling \nsurface high pressure sets up in the vicinity of the Great Lakes and \nsoutheast Canada for late week. Our POP drops off with the change in \nair mass, but some light precip won't be completely ruled out, \ndepending on the evolution of the persistent upper low/trough and \nany impulses rotating around it.",
